[SPEAKER_02]: I guess it comes together in a sense.

551
00:41:18,367 --> 00:41:20,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it depends, maybe, what you mean by taming.

552
00:41:20,629 --> 00:41:21,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you could...

553
00:41:21,990 --> 00:41:31,259
[SPEAKER_04]: It means, basically, sort of, not taming it, so it doesn't really turn towards craving as much, doesn't really attack you.

554
00:41:31,840 --> 00:41:34,402
[SPEAKER_04]: As much, it's actually calmer.

555
00:41:35,083 --> 00:41:40,847
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, even listens to what, what māna, what food māna presents itself, presents to it.

556
00:41:40,967 --> 00:41:41,167
[SPEAKER_04]: Sorry.

557
00:41:41,688 --> 00:41:45,691
[SPEAKER_03]: You can make a start, taming the mind, while there is still some sense of ownership there.

558
00:41:45,711 --> 00:41:46,011
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

559
00:41:46,111 --> 00:41:46,491
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

560
00:41:46,531 --> 00:41:50,254
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can tame your mind without necessarily abandoning ownership.

561
00:41:50,274 --> 00:41:55,298
[SPEAKER_04]: But obviously, abandoning the ownership would be much easier if the mind is already tamed.

562
00:41:55,898 --> 00:41:58,300
[SPEAKER_04]: And taming of the mind is what satyaṃ samādhi is.

563
00:41:59,081 --> 00:41:59,321
[SPEAKER_04]: So,

564
00:42:00,130 --> 00:42:01,111
[SPEAKER_04]: Want to practice samādhi?

565
00:42:01,131 --> 00:42:02,832
[SPEAKER_04]: Want to practice concentration?

566
00:42:02,892 --> 00:42:03,633
[SPEAKER_04]: Inverted commas?

567
00:42:03,913 --> 00:42:04,133
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

568
00:42:04,794 --> 00:42:10,679
[SPEAKER_04]: Guard the sense doors, dwell in solitude and observe the signs and features of the background moods that you are subjected to.

569
00:42:10,759 --> 00:42:13,561
[SPEAKER_04]: That is the definition of samādhi and composure.

570
00:42:14,943 --> 00:42:19,386
[SPEAKER_04]: Not ignore those things by focusing on one point in this nostril tip or something like that.

571
00:42:19,547 --> 00:42:20,507
[SPEAKER_04]: That's quite the opposite.

572
00:42:20,687 --> 00:42:21,989
[SPEAKER_04]: That's literally ignoring

573
00:42:22,822 --> 00:42:37,095
[SPEAKER_04]: The behavior of the king, and just focusing on the fork, or his hand, or his foot, or this, or that, so you don't see the unpleasant truth that's overwhelming you now, or he's about to punch you, he's about to throw you in jail.

574
00:42:37,696 --> 00:42:42,140
[SPEAKER_04]: But, as long as I focus on this one point, I won't be aware of the bars around me.

575
00:42:43,461 --> 00:42:44,642
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's not an escape.

576
00:42:45,678 --> 00:42:50,201
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it does provide some relief, but it requires constant maintenance, basically.

577
00:42:50,902 --> 00:42:51,482
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's... Yeah.

578
00:42:51,783 --> 00:42:58,388
[SPEAKER_04]: In the same sense, as we said, scratching, scratching the infected itch provides effectual relief, but it's making much worse.

579
00:42:58,408 --> 00:42:58,848
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

580
00:42:58,868 --> 00:42:59,989
[SPEAKER_04]: See, you are making this worse.

581
00:43:00,089 --> 00:43:05,453
[SPEAKER_04]: You're ignoring the king's hints and preferences.

582
00:43:06,273 --> 00:43:07,855
[SPEAKER_04]: So, he's going to be even more annoyed with you.

583
00:43:08,515 --> 00:43:10,196
[SPEAKER_04]: So, next time, he won't just put you in jail.

584
00:43:10,276 --> 00:43:12,258
[SPEAKER_04]: He's going to give you a hundred whips as well.

585
00:43:17,738 --> 00:43:18,498
[SPEAKER_04]: Sensuality worsens.

586
00:43:19,518 --> 00:43:36,523
[SPEAKER_04]: That's with preferences and chasing sensuality in that sense, or aversion, what makes things worse, makes your mind even wilder, even more bitter, even sharper, pain, even... People, they've been acting out of anger their whole life, you know, and they reach a certain age and just perpetually bitter and angry, even when there's no reason anymore.

587
00:43:37,223 --> 00:43:42,025
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, yeah, you really, you dig, dug into the bone now, which is scratching for these 30, 40, 50 years.

588
00:43:44,669 --> 00:43:50,895
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this single, one single phenomenon, the question refers to, is the sense of self.

589
00:43:50,955 --> 00:43:51,275
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

590
00:43:51,795 --> 00:43:54,077
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's what it is.

591
00:43:54,097 --> 00:44:05,647
[SPEAKER_04]: See, because of that sense of self, that you perpetuate, as we said, the gratuitous, like, despite all the signs and features of your mind, you're still like, yeah, this is, I am this, this is mine.

592
00:44:06,108 --> 00:44:08,330
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's why mind has the unchecked reign over you.

593
00:44:09,651 --> 00:44:11,973
[SPEAKER_03]: And anything that that mind changes, you'll be under, because

594
00:44:12,635 --> 00:44:14,595
[SPEAKER_04]: You took it through your ownership.

595
00:44:15,156 --> 00:44:29,839
[SPEAKER_04]: So, by removal, through seeing the independence of the mind, the anicca, the dukkha of it, all these characteristics, you will stop maintaining this idea that it's yours, that you are it.

596
00:44:30,239 --> 00:44:31,939
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's it.

597
00:44:32,219 --> 00:44:36,860
[SPEAKER_04]: That is unburdening you, factually, from the changes in that mind.

598
00:44:37,541 --> 00:44:39,621
[SPEAKER_04]: Whichever way it goes, it doesn't matter.

599
00:44:40,817 --> 00:44:44,400
[SPEAKER_04]: But for as long as there is that identity, whichever way it goes, the burden will be on you.

600
00:44:45,861 --> 00:44:56,610
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why that the precepts, the virtue, guiding the sense door, needs to be taken as a behavior regardless of the state of mind, regardless of what intentions you have and what you're inclined to do or not.

601
00:44:56,930 --> 00:44:58,332
[SPEAKER_04]: No, this will not be done.

602
00:44:58,572 --> 00:45:03,596
[SPEAKER_04]: So you find a common sort of principle that will now unite your behavior regardless of the mood.

603
00:45:04,796 --> 00:45:09,297
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so you cannot act your way out of a certain mood, because it is mood that determines the action.

604
00:45:09,477 --> 00:45:10,297
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

605
00:45:10,317 --> 00:45:11,958
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's the perversion and the order.

606
00:45:12,498 --> 00:45:19,179
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, seeing that, might also prevent a necessary action and just endure the mind as mind.

607
00:45:19,600 --> 00:45:19,800
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

608
00:45:20,520 --> 00:45:21,640
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, uninclined.

609
00:45:22,340 --> 00:45:25,101
[SPEAKER_04]: See, in ānāpānasati sutta, that's kind of the whole point.

610
00:45:25,961 --> 00:45:27,341
[SPEAKER_04]: Again, we say so many times.

611
00:45:27,541 --> 00:45:33,843
[SPEAKER_04]: How can you practice gladdening of the mind, experiencing the mind, expanding the mind, calming the mind, if you don't even see the mind?

612
00:45:35,143 --> 00:45:37,825
[SPEAKER_04]: And if you see the mind correctly, you would at least be a sotāpanna.

613
00:45:39,246 --> 00:45:49,993
[SPEAKER_04]: Thus, when a sotāpanna does ānāpānasati, sotāpanna or higher does ānāpānasati, he can, by that time, because he already has discerned his mind sufficiently, as this an independent phenomena that he is within.

614
00:45:51,535 --> 00:45:56,378
[SPEAKER_04]: And then he can, okay, now let me just experience the mind, regardless of the specific.

615
00:45:57,351 --> 00:45:58,591
[SPEAKER_04]: state that mind is.

616
00:45:59,371 --> 00:46:07,373
[SPEAKER_04]: Let me breathe in, let me breathe out while experiencing that mind, while re-familiarizing myself with the phenomenon of an enduring mind that I'm subjected to.

617
00:46:09,333 --> 00:46:11,014
[SPEAKER_04]: And let me let me gladden that mind.

618
00:46:12,134 --> 00:46:13,074
[SPEAKER_04]: Let me make it lighter.

619
00:46:14,614 --> 00:46:17,375
[SPEAKER_04]: Let me unincline it from these directions of sensuality.

620
00:46:18,535 --> 00:46:23,116
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, you're so skilled now to recognize these precursors to

621
00:46:24,273 --> 00:46:26,455
[SPEAKER_04]: Obvious kind of perceptions you can attend to.

622
00:46:26,475 --> 00:46:32,079
[SPEAKER_04]: You're so skilled in recognizing these behaviors of a king, even before king knows what he's about to do.

623
00:46:32,860 --> 00:46:36,303
[SPEAKER_04]: You already see, because you've watched it and learned so closely.

624
00:46:37,083 --> 00:46:40,766
[SPEAKER_04]: Not through examination with your eyes, but through, well, the dhamma eye.

625
00:46:41,927 --> 00:46:43,088
[SPEAKER_04]: See on the level of the mind.

626
00:46:43,469 --> 00:46:47,632
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, yeah, you could then, basically, your mind

627
00:46:49,708 --> 00:46:56,734
[SPEAKER_04]: wants to go here, wants to go there, but you are the one who sets up the direction and sends the mind in the right direction, in the right way.

628
00:46:56,754 --> 00:47:01,638
[SPEAKER_04]: That's like the taming of the elephant so that you can train the elephant, so that he actually starts listening.

629
00:47:02,859 --> 00:47:09,004
[SPEAKER_04]: So, your citta basically starts listening to what your well-restrained, thoroughly understood māna tells it to do.

630
00:47:10,005 --> 00:47:16,431
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what your intellect, what your recognition, what your kind of wisdom tells it to do, the mind that goes that way.

631
00:47:17,173 --> 00:47:18,554
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the practice of ānāpānasati.

632
00:47:18,574 --> 00:47:23,897
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what results in fortifying the four foundations of that right memory, mindfulness.

633
00:47:25,398 --> 00:47:28,800
[SPEAKER_04]: We're talking about gladdening the mind and so on, so... Liberating the mind.

634
00:47:28,840 --> 00:47:29,740
[SPEAKER_04]: Liberating the mind.

635
00:47:29,780 --> 00:47:30,941
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

636
00:47:31,161 --> 00:47:39,966
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can now, as I said, by being familiar with that phenomenon of enduring mind, discerning the slightest signs and features of it.

637
00:47:40,006 --> 00:47:43,388
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't need big coarse moods to see where the mind is.

638
00:47:45,122 --> 00:47:59,289
[SPEAKER_04]: The slightest signs and features, the slightest movements, so to speak, of neutral, undefined kind, it's enough for you to see the mind, to see the state of the mind, even before the mind is aware of it, even before it has assumed the direction and so on.

639
00:48:00,209 --> 00:48:03,811
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's pretty much, by that point, you'll be abandoning any five lower fetters.

640
00:48:06,112 --> 00:48:11,394
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that level of being, that level of requirement of mind leaning is gone now.

641
00:48:12,075 --> 00:48:14,756
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you got so used to seeing it even before, as I said,

642
00:48:15,416 --> 00:48:16,957
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, king doesn't move his little finger.

643
00:48:16,977 --> 00:48:19,238
[SPEAKER_04]: You already know where this would go.

644
00:48:19,338 --> 00:48:21,220
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you either allow it or not.

645
00:48:21,240 --> 00:48:35,748
[SPEAKER_04]: So, all these little signs and features and tokens, all these inclinations, all these nimittas, all these foretellers of mind is about to, you know, go and engage mentally, lean towards lust and then you have to endure the pain of it.

646
00:48:37,069 --> 00:48:37,870
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, you're seeing that.

647
00:48:37,890 --> 00:48:40,691
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, you're seeing that even before the mind recognized those things were there.

648
00:48:42,232 --> 00:48:43,913
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you turn it away.

649
00:48:44,053 --> 00:48:45,014
[SPEAKER_04]: You send it the other way.

650
00:48:46,564 --> 00:48:51,088
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly the practice of, oh, can the unreasonableness arise in me?

651
00:48:51,969 --> 00:48:57,573
[SPEAKER_04]: And a mind... and a skillful monk would recognize those little telltales.

652
00:48:59,575 --> 00:49:00,576
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, am I welcoming?

653
00:49:00,616 --> 00:49:02,397
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I with this unsuitable kind?

654
00:49:02,417 --> 00:49:06,161
[SPEAKER_04]: So, basically, refining the principle of skillful is skillful, unskillful, unskillful.

655
00:49:06,841 --> 00:49:11,145
[SPEAKER_04]: Sotāpanna hasn't understood that as a principle, but hasn't understood the

656
00:49:13,350 --> 00:49:20,336
[SPEAKER_04]: Kind of subtler implications of it, because if he did, he would abandon even that, and then he'd be an anāgāmī, or an āgāmī.

657
00:49:23,919 --> 00:49:32,665
[SPEAKER_02]: But when you say you recognize before the mind even sees or recognizes, so to say, how is that possible?

658
00:49:33,486 --> 00:49:40,492
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you recognize before it's present, so to say, or is it present for you before it's present for the mind, or...?

659
00:49:41,235 --> 00:49:44,217
[SPEAKER_04]: It's present for both, but you see the mind doesn't, because you see clearer.

660
00:49:47,078 --> 00:49:48,719
[SPEAKER_04]: It's possible, because you're not the mind.

661
00:49:48,739 --> 00:49:51,220
[SPEAKER_04]: Mind is a separate thing, enduring there, with your sense of self.

662
00:49:51,881 --> 00:50:01,066
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, obviously, if you're to the point of seeing the mind correctly, independently, that you, that we talk about, that sense of self, is your not-self.

663
00:50:01,886 --> 00:50:05,308
[SPEAKER_04]: But that creature is still there, as the suttas refer to.

664
00:50:06,349 --> 00:50:08,670
[SPEAKER_04]: That pūgala, that individual, is still there.

665
00:50:09,410 --> 00:50:13,311
[SPEAKER_04]: And he is the one who guards the mind, because that's pleasant here and now.

666
00:50:14,271 --> 00:50:20,212
[SPEAKER_04]: And he is the one who has liberated the mind, so where the aggregates break apart of that individual, there will be no more rebirth.

667
00:50:20,312 --> 00:50:23,573
[SPEAKER_04]: There will be no more taking up of things, because the mind has turned away from all of it.

668
00:50:23,813 --> 00:50:25,173
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you made it turn away.

669
00:50:26,233 --> 00:50:27,133
[SPEAKER_04]: You liberated it.

670
00:50:27,193 --> 00:50:29,454
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why you know that it's liberated, because you did it.

671
00:50:30,054 --> 00:50:31,034
[SPEAKER_04]: It didn't happen to you.

672
00:50:31,794 --> 00:50:32,774
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's what the Sutta says.

673
00:50:34,054 --> 00:50:37,055
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, Arahant, he knows, oh, it's liberated.

674
00:50:39,102 --> 00:50:40,622
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there is no doubt there anymore.

675
00:50:40,983 --> 00:50:42,563
[SPEAKER_04]: The mind is turned away, gone.

676
00:50:44,804 --> 00:50:50,465
[SPEAKER_04]: Through first taming it, then training it, then directing it, then gladdening it, then liberating it.

677
00:50:52,066 --> 00:50:53,706
[SPEAKER_04]: And he has no desire to return anymore.

678
00:50:54,706 --> 00:51:03,289
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the only reason he kept returning is because he wanted to be free from suffering, which was... and returning was what was making it suffer.

679
00:51:03,309 --> 00:51:04,449
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the vicious circle.

